Approval note (TK-20260928-8334)
Attached is a summary of the run results for the B-21786 batch; please approve the next-step handling recommendation:
Number of samples: 88; 6 samples with hemolysis index above the limit (>3), IDs S-014, S-027, S-041, S-052, S-063, S-079;
Standard curve correlation coefficient 0.9962; maximum five-point back-calculated deviation 11.4%;
Low-level QC deviation +8.2%, high-level QC deviation -11.6%; the two levels are in opposite directions, and one replicate well of the high level exceeds the 10% limit;
Within-plate CV 15.9%, slightly exceeding the 15% limit; positive and negative controls on the same plate meet criteria.
Per SOP, a QC deviation exceeding 10% or a CV exceeding 15% means this batch fails QC; therefore, this batch fails.
The key point is that the deviations at the two QC levels are in opposite directions (one positive and one negative), which is inconsistent with the typical manifestation of overall reagent degradation or systematic instrument drift and cannot be attributed to a single cause; at the same time, 6 samples on the same plate have a hemolysis index above the limit. My recommendation is not to rerun directly, nor to release after recalibration first; instead, initiate a deviation investigation and clarify both the sampling steps for the hemolyzed samples and the loading records for that plate before deciding.
Note: the maintenance work order for DEV-9910 has been forwarded to the Equipment Group and is unrelated to this decision; the report that a logistics dispatcher is chasing is for another batch—do not mix them up.

